From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 14:51:06 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vhost_xen: Bind the guest by name, not only by the lowest
 domid

vhost_xen keeps one guest domid for the whole module, and since the
previous patch it binds the lowest live domid whose device model is
running. That relies on DomA, the only guest that uses vhost, being
created first. Destroying and re-creating DomA while DomU stays up breaks
it: DomA comes back with a domid above DomU's, and the rule binds DomU.

Seen on a Raspberry Pi 5 after xl destroy DomA: the binding moved to
DomU ("Set new domid: 9") as soon as DomA was gone, the re-created DomA
(domid 15) stayed in its boot animation with

  vhost_xen_map_desc: Failed to map pages from dom9 (ret=-22)

and DomU ended up corrupted (a stream of systemd-coredump messages, no
ssh), most likely because DomA's vhost-net and vhost-vsock mapped DomU's
memory at DomA's addresses. Writing 15 to guest_domid by hand let DomA
boot.

Add a guest_name module parameter, "DomA" by default, set at boot. Only a
candidate whose /local/domain/<domid>/name is guest_name is bound, and it
wins over the lowest-domid rule; of two (an old DomA whose tree is not
removed yet, and the new one) the higher domid, the newer. With no such
candidate, for example right after DomA is destroyed, the existing rule
keeps the last binding instead of moving it to DomU. A name read that
fails with -EACCES (xenstore VM separation, as with the liveness probe)
leaves the candidate to the lowest-domid rule, so a DomD that cannot read
names behaves as before; any other failure skips the candidate, so that a
transient error reading DomU's name while DomA is gone cannot bind DomU.
An empty guest_name turns the check off.

Keeping the binding while DomA is gone is now the common case, and the
warning it logs fired on every device-model write: 150 lines over one
destroy and re-create of DomA. Log it once each time the scan stops
qualifying a candidate.

Checked on the same board: after xl destroy DomA the binding stayed on
the old domid, with one warning line, the re-created DomA was bound
within a second of its device model starting and booted to its home
screen, and DomU, restarted afterwards, stayed up and kept DomA's
binding. An earlier revision (the lowest named domid, every failed name
read left to the old rule, every warning logged) passed the same steps
with five DomU restarts; the handling of failed reads other than -EACCES
is checked on a PC only.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-5-5
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [downstream fork; drivers/vhost/xen.c exists only in the xen-troops/linux fork, not in mainline]
---
 drivers/vhost/xen.c | 76 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 66 insertions(+), 10 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/vhost/xen.c b/drivers/vhost/xen.c
index b924c65..fb91329 100644
--- a/drivers/vhost/xen.c
+++ b/drivers/vhost/xen.c
@@ -28,6 +28,18 @@ module_param(guest_domid, ushort, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(guest_domid,
 		 "Current guest domid used for Xen mappings (writable for manual recovery)");
 
+/*
+ * The guest vhost serves. vhost_xen keeps a single guest domid for the whole
+ * module, so it must never end up on a guest that does not use vhost: DomA's
+ * vhost-vsock and vhost-net would then map that guest's memory. Binding by name
+ * keeps it on DomA when DomA is re-created with a domid above another guest's.
+ * Empty: the lowest domid whose device model runs, as before.
+ */
+static char *guest_name = "DomA";
+module_param(guest_name, charp, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(guest_name,
+		 "Name of the guest vhost serves, set at boot (empty: lowest domid with a running device model)");
+
 static bool nogrant;
 module_param(nogrant, bool, 0444);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(nogrant, "Disable Xen grant mappings");
@@ -509,12 +521,18 @@ static const struct vhost_xen_ops vhost_xen_foreign_ops = {
 	.unmap_pages = vhost_xen_foreign_unmap_pages,
 };
 
+/*
+ * The kept binding whose warning was logged. The watch callbacks run one at a
+ * time on the xenwatch thread, so it needs no lock.
+ */
+static domid_t warned_domid = DOMID_INVALID;
+
 static void vhost_xen_get_guest_domid(struct xenbus_watch *watch,
 		const char *path, const char *token)
 {
 	char **dm_dir;
 	unsigned int i, n = 0;
-	domid_t new_domid = DOMID_INVALID;
+	domid_t new_domid = DOMID_INVALID, named_domid = DOMID_INVALID;
 
 	dm_dir = xenbus_directory(XBT_NIL, "device-model", "", &n);
 	if (IS_ERR(dm_dir))
@@ -586,13 +604,14 @@ static void vhost_xen_get_guest_domid(struct xenbus_watch *watch,
 			continue;
 
 		dom_dir = xenbus_directory(XBT_NIL, dm_path, "", &m);
-		kfree(dm_path);
 		if (!IS_ERR(dom_dir)) {
 			kfree(dom_dir);
 		} else if (PTR_ERR(dom_dir) == -ENOENT) {
 			/* Positively gone: the toolstack removed the tree. Skip. */
+			kfree(dm_path);
 			continue;
 		}
+
 		/*
 		 * Any other error (notably -EACCES: this DomD is built with
 		 * DISTRO_FEATURES += vmsep, so xenstore VM separation can deny
@@ -611,18 +630,46 @@ static void vhost_xen_get_guest_domid(struct xenbus_watch *watch,
 		 * way a highest-domid rule would (an earlier revision of this
 		 * change used one; the symptom was seen on hardware).
 		 *
-		 * Known limitation: if DomA were destroyed and re-created while
-		 * DomU stayed up, DomA would come back with a HIGHER domid and
-		 * this rule would keep pointing at DomU. That case is out of
-		 * scope by design -- destroying DomA is not supported on this
-		 * platform (the single global binding below is exactly why) --
-		 * but a topology with several vhost guests would need the domid
-		 * to be a per-device property instead of a module-global.
+		 * That order does not survive DomA being destroyed and
+		 * re-created while DomU stays up: DomA comes back with a HIGHER
+		 * domid. So only a guest whose name is guest_name is bound, and
+		 * it wins over the rule above; of two such guests (an old DomA
+		 * whose tree the toolstack has not removed yet, and the new one)
+		 * the higher domid, the newer. Only -EACCES (the same vmsep case)
+		 * leaves a guest whose name cannot be read to the lowest-domid
+		 * rule: any other failure skips it, so that a transient error
+		 * reading DomU's name while DomA is gone cannot bind DomU.
+		 * A topology with several vhost guests would still need the
+		 * domid to be a per-device property instead of a module-global.
 		 */
+		if (guest_name && guest_name[0]) {
+			char *name = (char *)xenbus_read(XBT_NIL, dm_path, "name", NULL);
+			bool match = false;
+
+			if (IS_ERR(name)) {
+				if (PTR_ERR(name) != -EACCES) {
+					kfree(dm_path);
+					continue;
+				}
+			} else {
+				match = !strcmp(name, guest_name);
+				kfree(name);
+				if (!match) {
+					kfree(dm_path);
+					continue;
+				}
+			}
+			if (match && (named_domid == DOMID_INVALID || domid > named_domid))
+				named_domid = domid;
+		}
+		kfree(dm_path);
+
 		if (new_domid == DOMID_INVALID || domid < new_domid)
 			new_domid = domid;
 
 	}
+	if (named_domid != DOMID_INVALID)
+		new_domid = named_domid;
 
 	/*
 	 * Only tear the binding down when the directory is genuinely empty. If it
@@ -631,10 +678,19 @@ static void vhost_xen_get_guest_domid(struct xenbus_watch *watch,
 	 * known good binding is strictly better than clearing it: clearing makes
 	 * every subsequent vhost_xen_map_desc() fail for all guests at once.
 	 */
+	if (new_domid != DOMID_INVALID)
+		warned_domid = DOMID_INVALID;
 	if (new_domid == DOMID_INVALID && n > 0) {
-		if (guest_domid != DOMID_INVALID)
+		/*
+		 * Once each time the scan stops qualifying anything: while DomA
+		 * is gone, every device-model write lands here (150 lines over
+		 * one destroy and re-create of DomA on a Raspberry Pi 5).
+		 */
+		if (guest_domid != DOMID_INVALID && guest_domid != warned_domid) {
 			pr_warn("%s: no qualifying device-model among %u entries; keeping dom%u\n",
 				__func__, n, guest_domid);
+			warned_domid = guest_domid;
+		}
 	} else if (new_domid != guest_domid) {
 		WRITE_ONCE(guest_domid, new_domid);
 		if (new_domid == DOMID_INVALID)
